<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00649</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</md:title>
    <md:uuid>12605343-ef87-4221-a0ea-e7dd720ffd44</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Here are the graphs of the inequalities in this system:</para>
      <!--BEGIN
      SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS -->
      <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;\lt\;y\\y\geq\;-2x\;-\;6\end{array}\right.\)</para>
      <!--END
      SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS -->
      <!--BEGIN
      SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS-->


      <media alt="A graph of two intersecting inequalities on a coordinate plane.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_17_03_01.svg" />
      </media>


      <para>Decide whether each point is a solution to the system. Be prepared to explain how you
        know.</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((3,-5)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No. \((3,-5)\) is in the shaded region containing the solutions to one of the
            inequalities but not in the region where the shading overlaps.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((0,5)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes. \((0,5)\) is in the region where the solutions to the two inequalities overlap.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((-6,6)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes. (\(-6,6)\) is on the solid boundary of \(y\geq-2x-6\) because \(0=-2(-3)−6\),
            and it is within the shaded region representing solutions to \(x&lt;y\). </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((3,3)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No. While \((3,3)\) is clearly a solution to \(y\geq-2x-6\), it is on the dashed
            boundary of \(x &lt; y\). The statement \(3&lt;3\) is not true. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((-2,-2)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No, because \(-2&lt;-2\) is not true. Even though it is on the solid boundary of
            one, it is on the dashed boundary of the other. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
